<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SOURCE OF TRUTH KEYWORDS: entitlements, audio-input, hardened-runtime,
    microphone, com.apple.security.device.audio-input
  WHAT:  The macOS entitlements the signed bundle carries.
  WHY:   THIS FILE IS WHY THE MICROPHONE WORKS. Signing with a real identity
         turns on Hardened Runtime, and under Hardened Runtime the microphone
         is blocked at the OS level unless the app declares
         `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` — no matter what the user has
         toggled in Privacy & Security.

         The failure that produced this file: the app was signed with an
         identity, gained Hardened Runtime, carried no entitlements, and then
         recorded pure silence. Every visible signal said it should work —
         Murmur was listed and enabled under Microphone, the device opened, the
         pipeline ran — and the transcript came back empty. The permission
         check even reported NotDetermined, because a Hardened Runtime process
         without the entitlement cannot meaningfully ask.

         Deliberately minimal. The app is NOT sandboxed, so network access,
         the model download and the application-support directory need nothing
         declared. Accessibility is a TCC grant rather than an entitlement.
         Every entry here has to earn its place; an entitlement that is not
         needed is attack surface that is not needed.
  WHERE: Referenced by bundle.macOS.entitlements in tauri.conf.json. Asserted by
         the bundle test in src/bootstrap.rs so it cannot be silently dropped.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Microphone capture through CoreAudio (cpal). Without this the app
         records silence under Hardened Runtime. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
